<time datetime="2023-06-12">June 12, 2023</time>
<article id="HTTP" class="WebBits">
    <h2>LEARN: HTTP</h2>
    <div class="columns">
        <p class="col-d-6">
            <strong>HyperText Transport Protocol</strong>,
            <abbr title="HyperText Transport Protocol">HTTP</abbr>, is an
            application-layer protocol responsible for retrieving and sending
            web pages. Http gives the world wide web the ability to view/host
            website content regardless of the location of the request. Without
            it, websites cannot exist as we know them today.
        </p>
        <aside class="exampleBlue col-d-6">
            <p>
                By default, http operates on port 80 via
                <abbr title="Transmission Control Protocol">TCP</abbr> for client
                requests and server responses.
            </p>
        </aside>
        <aside class="examplePink col-d-6">
            <p>
                Multi-server website hosts are called web farms and are composed
                of three main server types:
            </p>
            <ul>
                <li>
                    <strong>Frontend server:</strong> A server hosting the website's
                    content files such as HTML, CSS, and JavaScript files.
                </li>
                <li>
                    <strong>Backend server:</strong> A server dedicated to
                    application-specific tasks such as database API access and
                    dynamic content rendering.
                </li>
                <li>
                    <strong>Database server:</strong> A server containing customer
                    or sensitive data for the website in secure database tables.
                </li>
            </ul>
        </aside>
        <p class="col-d-6">
            Http traverses the web in network packet containers, called frames,
            via <em>HTTP/2</em>. <em>HTTP/3</em> was published 06-06-2022 by the
            <abbr title="Internet Engineering Task Force">IETF</abbr>.<sup>1</sup>
        </p>
        <p class="col-d-10">
            Commonly, http requests are made by a web browser. The request
            reaches the website's frontend &lpar;via routers and switches&rpar;,
            which may be an internet-hosted web server or a corporation's server
            bank. Multiple servers can work together to handle process volume
            and speed up response time.
        </p>
    </div>
    <hr class="dl">
    <dl>
        <dt>Frames</dt>
        <dd>An independent chunk of data sent over a network.</dd>
        <dt>HyperText Transport Protocol</dt>
        <dd>The data transfer protocol used on the World Wide Web.</dd>
        <dt>HyperText Transport Protocol/2</dt>
        <dd>Emplaces http in frames for reduced latency.</dd>
        <dt>HyperText Transport Protocol/3</dt>
        <dd>
            The third major version of http using
            <abbr title="Quick UDP Internet Connections">QUIC</abbr>, a
            multiplexed transport protocol built on
            <abbr title="User Datagram Protocol">UDP</abbr>.<sup>1</sup>
        </dd>
        <dt>Server</dt>
        <dd>
            A computer dedicated to running programs that provide services to
            other programs or devices, either in the same computer or over a
            computer network.
        </dd>
        <dt>Transmission Control Protocol &lpar;TCP&rpar;</dt>
        <dd>Ensures reliable data exchange between devices.</dd>
        <dt>Web Browser</dt>
        <dd>
            A software application that allows a user to access and view
            websites on the World Wide Web.
        </dd>
        <dt>Web Farm</dt>
        <dd>
            Computing a large website that uses two or more servers to handle
            user requests.
        </dd>
        <dt>Website</dt>
        <dd>
            A collection of interlinked web pages on the World Wide Web that are
            typically accessible from the same base
            <abbr title="Uniform Resource Locator">URL</abbr> and reside on the
            same server.
        </dd>
    </dl>
    <p class="citations">
        <strong>Citations:</strong><br>
        <sup>[1]</sup>
